# .graphifyignore (or your indexer's equivalent) — what the repo INDEX must not read.
# Copy to your repo root and edit. This controls the index only; it never affects git.
#
# ── MATCHER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read your indexer's CURRENT docs before trusting anything here: matcher syntax
# and .gitignore interaction DIFFER BETWEEN VERSIONS of the same tool, and an
# exclusion file written for an older version can silently change what is indexed
# after an upgrade. The rules below are gitignore-pathspec, which is what most
# indexers use today:
#   · a pattern with NO slash matches a BASENAME at any depth  (.env, *.swp)
#   · a pattern WITH a slash is anchored to this file's directory  (docs/private/)
#   · a LEADING slash anchors to that root                        (/local.toml)
#   · `**` is any-depth; `*` does NOT cross `/`
#   · a TRAILING slash means directories only
#   · `!` re-includes; LAST MATCH WINS, so negations must FOLLOW the broad exclude
#
# ── INTERACTION WITH .gitignore ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Most indexers now MERGE .gitignore (every directory's, nested ones included)
# with this file, evaluating this file LAST so it wins conflicts — but it can only
# ever EXCLUDE MORE. A `!` here CANNOT re-include something .gitignore already
# excluded. Check any rescue with:  git check-ignore -v <path>
# If git excludes it, your rescue is inert.
#   Effective scope = whole repo − .gitignore − this file − the tool's built-ins.
#
# Your indexer almost certainly prunes deps/build/cache dirs (node_modules, .venv,
# build, dist, target, its own output dir, caches) automatically. Don't re-list them.

# ── Secrets and secrets-adjacent — the reason this file is not optional ──────
# An index is a DERIVED ARTIFACT that can be read by an assistant, copied, or fed
# to a hosted service. Keep credentials out of it even when they are gitignored.
#
# ⚠️ MATCH ON SHAPE AND PATH, NOT ON THE WORD. A pattern like `**/*secret*` or
# `**/*token*` also excludes the file that DOCUMENTS secrets and the module that
# HANDLES tokens — e.g. a `06-secrets.md` chapter, an `api/token.py`. You lose
# exactly the architecture you most wanted indexed, and nothing tells you.
# Prefer concrete filenames/extensions and real credential directories; add a
# name-based pattern only after checking what it catches:
#     git ls-files | grep -i secret
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.keystore
.netrc
.npmrc
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
# credential DIRECTORIES (paths, not name substrings):
.aws/
.ssh/
.gnupg/
secrets/

# ── Local/machine state that is noise, not architecture ─────────────────────
.DS_Store
._*
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# ── Vendored / generated code — usually noise, occasionally the point ────────
# Exclude by default; if generated code IS what you need indexed, keep it and see
# your indexer's flag for including git-ignored files.
vendor/
generated/

# ── Archived or superseded content ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Old material an assistant will otherwise cite as if current — a common source of
# confidently-wrong answers. Case-insensitive, any depth:
*[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee]*

# ── Large fixtures/data with no semantic value ───────────────────────────────
# The keep-some-of-a-directory idiom: exclude the contents, then re-include what
# matters. Works ONLY if .gitignore doesn't already exclude them (see above).
# test-fixtures/*
# !test-fixtures/README.md
# !test-fixtures/manifest.txt
